DGL: say whether a key was taken, so macOS can give back the ones that were not.

An embedded plugin view is the host's first responder for as long as the plugin
window has the focus - on VST3 DPF even asks for that itself, in
DistrhoUIVST3.cpp's onFocus. Cocoa's keyDown: then ends at that view, and pugl's
implementation of it neither consumed the key nor passed it on: it dispatched the
event and returned. So every key reaching the plugin window died there, whether the
plugin wanted it or not, and the host lost its own key commands and its on-screen
keyboard for as long as the editor was in front. Reported against SQ8L in Cubase
and REAPER on macOS, and true of any DPF plugin.

Nothing below the widgets knew: Window::PrivateData::onPuglKey already had the
answer - TopLevelWidget::onKeyboard returns whether the widget used the key - and
threw it away. This returns it, and the pugl event callback turns "no widget took
it" into PUGL_FAILURE, which the matching mac.m patch reads as "hand it to the next
responder".

PUGL_FAILURE is documented as a non-fatal failure and is the only status that
carries that meaning, but it is also what puglUpdate hands back to its caller on
X11, where a key event is dispatched through the same return path. So it is
macOS-only here: Windows and X11 keep their status exactly as it was, and have
their own routes for an unwanted key anyway (the host's own VST2/VST3 key channel,
which DPF already answers from the return value of UI::onKeyboard).
diff --git a/dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.cpp b/dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.cpp
index 7198ca3..9df6e6b 100644
--- a/dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.cpp
+++ b/dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.cpp
@@ -769,12 +769,15 @@ void Window::PrivateData::onPuglFocus(const bool focus, const CrossingMode mode)
     self->onFocus(focus, mode);
 }
 
-void Window::PrivateData::onPuglKey(const Widget::KeyboardEvent& ev)
+bool Window::PrivateData::onPuglKey(const Widget::KeyboardEvent& ev)
 {
     DGL_DBGp("onPuglKey : %i %u %u\n", ev.press, ev.key, ev.keycode);
 
     if (modal.child != nullptr)
-        return modal.child->focus();
+    {
+        modal.child->focus();
+        return true;
+    }
 
 #ifndef DPF_TEST_WINDOW_CPP
     FOR_EACH_TOP_LEVEL_WIDGET_INV(rit)
@@ -782,9 +785,11 @@ void Window::PrivateData::onPuglKey(const Widget::KeyboardEvent& ev)
         TopLevelWidget* const widget(*rit);
 
         if (widget->isVisible() && widget->onKeyboard(ev))
-            break;
+            return true;
     }
 #endif
+
+    return false;
 }
 
 void Window::PrivateData::onPuglText(const Widget::CharacterInputEvent& ev)
@@ -1053,7 +1058,16 @@ PuglStatus Window::PrivateData::puglEventCallback(PuglView* const view, const Pu
             ev.mod |= kModifierShift;
         }
 
+       #ifdef DISTRHO_OS_MAC
+        // A key no widget took is the host's. Only macOS routes it back from here: the Cocoa
+        // view is the first responder and nothing above it ever sees the event, so PUGL_FAILURE
+        // is how mac.m is told to hand it on (see the keyDown: patch). Windows and X11 keep
+        // their own paths and their own status, which callers of puglUpdate do read.
+        if (! pData->onPuglKey(ev))
+            return PUGL_FAILURE;
+       #else
         pData->onPuglKey(ev);
+       #endif
         break;
     }
 
diff --git a/dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.hpp b/dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.hpp
index 3883184..d566a2d 100644
--- a/dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.hpp
+++ b/dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.hpp
@@ -200,7 +200,9 @@ struct Window::PrivateData : IdleCallback {
     void onPuglExpose();
     void onPuglClose();
     void onPuglFocus(bool focus, CrossingMode mode);
-    void onPuglKey(const Widget::KeyboardEvent& ev);
+    // true if a widget took the key. macOS needs to know: a key no widget wanted has to go
+    // back up the responder chain to the host, and nothing below this can tell.
+    bool onPuglKey(const Widget::KeyboardEvent& ev);
     void onPuglText(const Widget::CharacterInputEvent& ev);
     void onPuglMouse(const Widget::MouseEvent& ev);
     void onPuglMotion(const Widget::MotionEvent& ev);
